{"id":"a10","level":2,"levelName":"Ulpan Alef","n":10,"of":10,"title":"Counting","titleHe":"מִסְפָּרִים","goal":"Count to ten — and pick the right set of numbers.","sections":[{"type":"text","body":"Hebrew numbers have two sets, and the one you use depends on the gender of what you're counting. This catches every learner: the form that looks feminine, ending in ־ָה, is the one used with masculine nouns."},{"type":"table","caption":"One to ten","head":["","with feminine nouns","with masculine nouns"],"rows":[["1","אַחַת","אֶחָד"],["2","שְׁתַּיִם","שְׁנַיִם"],["3","שָׁלוֹשׁ","שְׁלוֹשָׁה"],["4","אַרְבַּע","אַרְבָּעָה"],["5","חָמֵשׁ","חֲמִשָּׁה"],["6","שֵׁשׁ","שִׁשָּׁה"],["7","שֶׁבַע","שִׁבְעָה"],["8","שְׁמוֹנֶה","שְׁמוֹנָה"],["9","תֵּשַׁע","תִּשְׁעָה"],["10","עֶשֶׂר","עֲשָׂרָה"]]},{"type":"tip","body":"Counting out loud — one, two, three with nothing being counted — uses the feminine set. So does giving a phone number."},{"type":"tip","body":"One is the exception to the exception: it follows its noun like an adjective. סֵפֶר אֶחָד, one book. Every other number comes first: שְׁלוֹשָׁה סְפָרִים."}],"vocab":[{"he":"אֶחָד","en":"one (m.)","translit":"echad"},{"he":"שְׁנַיִם","en":"two (m.)","translit":"shnayim"},{"he":"שְׁלוֹשָׁה","en":"three (m.)","translit":"shlosha"},{"he":"אַרְבָּעָה","en":"four (m.)","translit":"arba'a"},{"he":"חֲמִשָּׁה","en":"five (m.)","translit":"chamisha"},{"he":"עֲשָׂרָה","en":"ten (m.)","translit":"asara"},{"he":"מִסְפָּר","en":"number","translit":"mispar"}],"exercises":[{"type":"choice","q":"What does this mean?","options":["this","explains","tree","one (m.)"],"correct":3,"prompt":"אֶחָד","translit":"echad"},{"type":"choice","q":"What does this mean?","options":["accepted, customary","table","hospital","two (m.)"],"correct":3,"prompt":"שְׁנַיִם","translit":"shnayim"},{"type":"choice","q":"What does this mean?","options":["three (m.)","year","city","house"],"correct":0,"prompt":"שְׁלוֹשָׁה","translit":"shlosha"},{"type":"choice","q":"What does 